;;|| NEXT >
;;
(begin-tx "DALOS-G2f -- enforce-one recovers from a failed enforce, NOT from a native fault")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 10000000)(env-gas 0)
;;==== DALOS-G2f -- enforce-one recovers from a failed enforce, NOT from a native fault · 01 · namespace ==== 
(print "--- [DALOS-G2f -- enforce-one recovers from a failed enforce, NOT from a native fault · 01 · namespace] ---")
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
(print "--- [DALOS-G2f . 01 . the limit of enforce-one] ---")
;;DALOS-G2c pinned that enforce-one SWALLOWS a nested branch's message and reports its own. This
;;pins the other half, which is easy to assume wrongly in the opposite direction: enforce-one is
;;not a general try/catch. It recovers from a failed `enforce` and from nothing else. A native
;;fault -- an out-of-bounds index, say -- propagates straight through it and kills the transaction.
;;
;;Why it matters here rather than as a language curiosity. GAS_PAYER validates the transaction's
;;forms as three enforce-one branches, and each branch is a (fold (and) true [...]) that indexes
;;exec-lines -- (at 0 …) in case 1, (at 1 …) in cases 2 and 3. `fold` builds its whole list before
;;`and` runs, so those indexes are NOT guarded by the (= n 1) / (= n 2) conjuncts sitting beside
;;them. What actually keeps the gas station safe is enforce-one's ORDERING: for n=1 case 1 succeeds
;;and the later branches are never evaluated, so (at 1 exec-lines) is never reached.
;;
;;That is a real guarantee, but it is a load-bearing accident of branch order, not a check. Reorder
;;those branches, or hand GAS_PAYER an EMPTY exec-lines, and case 1's own (at 0 exec-lines) faults
;;with a raw index error that enforce-one cannot absorb -- asserted below. Empty transaction code
;;is not reachable in practice, which is why this is pinned as a boundary rather than filed as a
;;defect. The detector for the shape is `python3 tools/_foldeager.py` ([index] section).
(map print
    [
        ;;half one: a failed enforce inside a branch IS recovered -- the next branch is tried
        (expect "<<DALOS-G2f>> enforce-one recovers from a failed enforce in an earlier branch"
            true (enforce-one "outer" [(enforce false "branch one fails") true]))
        ;;half two: a native fault in an earlier branch is NOT recovered. Note the message that
        ;;arrives is the INDEX error, not "outer" -- so enforce-one did not even get to report.
        (expect-failure "<<DALOS-G2f>> ...but a native index fault propagates straight through it"
            "Array index out of bounds. Length (1), Index (5)"
            (enforce-one "outer" [(enforce (= "x" (at 5 ["a"])) "branch one") true]))
    ]
)
(commit-tx)

